Table: Seat

+-------------+---------+
| Column Name | Type    |
+-------------+---------+
| id          | int     |
| student     | varchar |
+-------------+---------+
id is the primary key (unique value) column for this table.
Each row of this table indicates the name and the ID of a student.
The ID sequence always starts from 1 and increments continuously.

Write a solution to swap the seat id of every two consecutive students. If the number of students is odd, the id of the last student is not swapped.

Return the result table ordered by id in ascending order.

The result format is in the following example.

using mod(id, 2) or %
the mod function or the % operator is used to calculate the modules (remainder) of a division operation
mod(id, 2) = 1 and id != (select max(id) from Seat): this condition identifies odd id, except the very last one in the tables
    -> For these student, the id is incremented by 1 (id+1) to swap them with the next student
mod(id, 2) = 0: This condition identifies all even id
    -> For these student, the id is decremented by 1 (id-1) to swap them with the previou student
else id: handles the final odd id if the total number of students is odd, leaving id unchanged.

-- Write your PostgreSQL query statement below
select 
    case
        when mod(id, 2) = 1 and id != (select max(id) from Seat) then id + 1
        when mod(id, 2) = 0 then id - 1
        else id
    end as id,
    student
from Seat
order by id